<p>Walker measured 5-foot-9¼ and 211 pounds at the 2022 NFL Scouting Combine, with a 9½-inch hand. He ran the 40 in <strong>4.38 seconds</strong>, then added a 34-inch vertical and a 10-foot-2-inch broad jump. He skipped the three-cone drill. Coming off a junior season at Michigan State with 1,725 yards from scrimmage and 19 touchdowns, he went to Seattle in the second round, 41st overall.</p>
<p>On their own, the jumps are solid rather than spectacular. The 40 is the number that matters, and it matters <em>because of the weight attached to it</em>. A 4.38 from a 190-pound receiver is fast. A 4.38 from a 211-pound running back is a different kind of problem for a defense.</p>
<h3>Why weight changes what a 40 time means</h3>
<p>Scouts use a simple formula called <strong>speed score</strong> to put a 40 time in context: weight × 200, divided by the 40 time raised to the fourth power. It rewards big backs who still run fast. Roughly 100 is average for a running back prospect, and anything above 110 is considered elite.</p>
