<p>Those 665 yards are the most in NFL history by a player through his first five games with a new team, per ESPN. The Chiefs also note he's one of only four players ever to reach 660 rushing yards and six rushing touchdowns through a team's first five games. The other three are Terrell Davis, Jim Brown and O.J. Simpson. His most recent outing, a 22-6 win over the Chargers, was a 19-carry, 128-yard, two-touchdown game that included a 33-yard score in the first quarter.</p>
<h2>Where the Combine Shows Up: Yards After Contact</h2>
<p>The single most telling number in Walker's season is <strong>369 yards after contact</strong>, about 55% of his rushing total. More than half of what he gains comes after a defender has already touched him. Add the league-leading 52 broken tackles and the pattern is clear.</p>
<p>That's what a high speed score looks like on the field. Momentum is mass times velocity, and Walker brings both. A 4.38 over 40 yards is an <em>average</em> of roughly 8.4 meters per second, and his peak speed is higher than that. Pair that with 211 pounds and an arm tackle from a defensive back is often not enough to stop the play. The weight is what keeps him upright through contact; the speed is what turns a broken tackle into a 20-yard gain instead of a 4-yard one.</p>
